<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.1.2: Recognizing Structure in Perfect-Square Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02333</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.1.2: Recognizing Structure in Perfect-Square Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>acc10f7e-6d2f-4dd2-a1d4-a709485e0549</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">1. Each expression is written as the product of factors. Write an equivalent expression in standard form.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">\((3x)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\(9x^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">\(7x \cdot 7x\)</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">\(49x^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">\((x+4)(x+4)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">\(x^2+8x+16\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">\((x+1)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">\(x^2+2x+1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00018">\((x−7)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00021">\(x^2−14x+49\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00022">\((x+n)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00025">\(x^2+2nx+n^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00026">2. Why do you think the following expressions can be described as <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">perfect square</term>s?</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">\(x^2+6x+9\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x^2−16x+64\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x^2+ \frac {1}{3}x+ \frac {1}{36}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00028">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00030">If these expressions are written in factored form, each one is the square of a linear expression:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">\((x+3)^2\), \((x−8)^2\), and \((x+\frac {1}{6})^2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <section id="sect-00003">
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <exercise id="_98bb2177-78bd-418f-8bce-0281b92105e1">
            <problem id="prob-00008">
              <para id="para-00032">Write each expression in factored form.</para>
              <para id="para-00033">\(x^4 - 30x^2 + 225\)</para>
              <para id="para-00034">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00008">
              <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00036">\((x^2 - 15)^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!-- End CTA block -->
          <exercise id="_98f46003-e34f-4b77-8941-de4c32937d47">
            <problem id="prob-00009">
              <para id="para-00037">\(x + 14\sqrt{x} + 49\)</para>
              <para id="para-00038">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00009">
              <para id="para-00039">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00040">\((\sqrt{x} + 7)^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!-- End CTA block -->
          <exercise id="_37387496-60e5-4afe-95cc-4ab2cfee8365">
            <problem id="prob-00010">
              <para id="para-00041">\(5^{2x} + 6 \cdot 5^x + 9\)</para>
              <para id="para-00042">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00010">
              <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00044">\((5^x + 3)^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!-- End CTA block -->
        </section>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00045">Which of the following is equivalent to \((x-4)^2\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Creating Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
        <para id="para-00046">A <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">perfect square trinomial</term> is created by multiplying a binomial by itself.</para>
        <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00048">Write \((x-2)^2\) as an expanded polynomial in standard form:</para>
        <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Multiply the binomial by itself. \((x-2)(x-2)\)</para>
        <para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">distributive property</term> to expand (FOIL). \(x^2-2x-2x+4\)</para>
        <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms. \(x^2-4x+4\)</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Creating Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
            <exercise id="exer-00008">
            <problem id="prob-00011">
              <para id="para-00052">Write \((x+5)^2\) as an expanded polynomial in standard form.</para>
              <para id="para-00053">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00011">
              <para id="para-00054">Here is how to expand the polynomial:</para>
              <para id="para-00055"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Multiply the binomial by itself. \((x+5)(x+5)\)</para>
              <para id="para-00056"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the distributive property (FOIL). \(x^2 +5x+5x+25\)</para>
              <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms. \(x^2+10x+25\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
